Awesome — let’s dive into implementing **ATA (Advanced Technology Attachment)** for your bootloader and kernel. ATA is the protocol used to communicate with IDE hard drives, and it works at a very low level, making it perfect for bare-metal systems.

---

## 🧠 What ATA Does

ATA lets you:
- Read and write sectors from a hard disk.
- Communicate directly with the disk controller via I/O ports.
- Use **PIO mode** (Programmed I/O) or **DMA** (Direct Memory Access). We’ll start with **PIO**, which is simpler.

---

## 🛠️ ATA PIO Read: Step-by-Step

### 🔹 ATA I/O Ports (Primary Bus)
| Port | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `0x1F0` | Data register (16-bit) |
| `0x1F1` | Error register |
| `0x1F2` | Sector count |
| `0x1F3` | LBA low |
| `0x1F4` | LBA mid |
| `0x1F5` | LBA high |
| `0x1F6` | Drive/head |
| `0x1F7` | Command/status |

---

### 🔹 Assembly Code to Read One Sector

```asm
read_sector:
    mov dx, 0x1F2        ; Sector count
    mov al, 1
    out dx, al

    mov dx, 0x1F3        ; LBA low
    mov al, [lba]
    out dx, al

    mov dx, 0x1F4        ; LBA mid
    mov al, [lba+1]
    out dx, al

    mov dx, 0x1F5        ; LBA high
    mov al, [lba+2]
    out dx, al

    mov dx, 0x1F6        ; Drive/head
    mov al, 0xE0         ; Master drive + LBA mode
    or al, [lba+3]       ; LBA bits 24–27
    out dx, al

    mov dx, 0x1F7        ; Command register
    mov al, 0x20         ; READ SECTORS command
    out dx, al

.wait:
    in al, dx
    test al, 0x08        ; Check DRQ bit
    jz .wait

    mov cx, 256          ; 256 words = 512 bytes
    mov di, buffer
.read_loop:
    in ax, 0x1F0
    stosw
    loop .read_loop
    ret
```

---

## 📦 Integration with Kernel

You can expose this ATA read function to your Fortran kernel via a C wrapper:

```c
